00:02:51.290 --> 00:02:53.640
the circumpolar ship traffic.

00:02:53.640 --> 00:02:56.610
And so this video
shows the recession--

00:02:56.610 --> 00:02:58.920
the annual recession and
progression of the polar ice

00:02:58.920 --> 00:02:59.940
cap.

00:02:59.940 --> 00:03:03.130
And the blue and yellow
tracks show ship traffic here.

00:03:03.130 --> 00:03:05.070
And one thing that Greg
noticed through this

00:03:05.070 --> 00:03:08.800
is, A, the obvious one is
that every summer the ice

00:03:08.800 --> 00:03:10.500
cap recedes a little
bit further than it

00:03:10.500 --> 00:03:15.660
did the year before and
that the polar vessels are

00:03:15.660 --> 00:03:17.690
able to traverse a
little bit further north.

00:03:17.690 --> 00:03:20.760
The keen eye among
you as well might

00:03:20.760 --> 00:03:24.510
have noticed some blue traces
that go right through the ice.

00:03:24.510 --> 00:03:26.160
Those are ice breakers.

00:03:26.160 --> 00:03:27.380
So you can do that.

00:03:27.380 --> 00:03:28.920
There's even one-- there it is.

00:03:28.920 --> 00:03:30.330
It goes right to the North Pole.

00:03:30.330 --> 00:03:33.540
That's a $25,000 per
person tourist ride

00:03:33.540 --> 00:03:36.090
