00:08:23.050 --> 00:08:24.970
And now to tell you
what that story actually

00:08:24.970 --> 00:08:26.750
is, Davida Herzl from Aclima.

00:08:26.750 --> 00:08:27.250
Please.

00:08:38.476 --> 00:08:40.059
DAVIDA HERZL: Thank
you so much, Chad.

00:08:40.059 --> 00:08:41.794
And thank you everybody.

00:08:44.520 --> 00:08:48.440
So air pollution is
now a global epidemic.

00:08:48.440 --> 00:08:52.520
92% of the world is
breathing unhealthy air.

00:08:52.520 --> 00:08:56.700
That translates to seven million
premature deaths per year,

00:08:56.700 --> 00:09:02.990
which is greater than homicides
and traffic accidents combined.

00:09:02.990 --> 00:09:07.280
And it's not just these
staggering numbers as

00:09:07.280 --> 00:09:09.860
related to mortality.

00:09:09.860 --> 00:09:12.590
Air pollution is affecting
quality of life for people

00:09:12.590 --> 00:09:14.210
every single day.

00:09:14.210 --> 00:09:17.390
It's connected not only
to asthma and other forms

00:09:17.390 --> 00:09:19.760
of respiratory disease
but increasingly

00:09:19.760 --> 00:09:22.130
a wide number of other
human health impacts

00:09:22.130 --> 00:09:24.170
like Alzheimer's, diabetes.

00:09:24.170 --> 00:09:28.560
It's even connected to higher
incidences of crime in cities.

00:09:28.560 --> 00:09:30.770
And this is costing
us big money.

00:09:30.770 --> 00:09:38.330
In the US alone, air pollution
cost us $866 billion last year.

00:09:38.330 --> 00:09:40.610
And to address this
problem, it really

00:09:40.610 --> 00:09:42.980
comes down to a simple mantra.

00:09:42.980 --> 00:09:46.670
We have to measure
what we manage

00:09:46.670 --> 00:09:49.030
and manage what we measure.

00:09:49.030 --> 00:09:51.920
But current measuring
approaches are really

00:09:51.920 --> 00:09:56.520
insufficient to address
the scale of the problem.

00:09:56.520 --> 00:09:59.960
It's challenging to understand
exactly where emissions

00:09:59.960 --> 00:10:02.870
are coming from and
who is being affected

00:10:02.870 --> 00:10:05.990
with the current model of
measurement infrastructure

00:10:05.990 --> 00:10:08.240
that is deployed across
the United States,

00:10:08.240 --> 00:10:10.580
but also around the
world, that relies

00:10:10.580 --> 00:10:13.400
on traditional
methods of measurement

00:10:13.400 --> 00:10:18.200
that translate into very large
equipment that's not connected

00:10:18.200 --> 00:10:20.450
to the internet, is
difficult to deploy.

00:10:20.450 --> 00:10:23.390
And so you end up with
a very sparse deployment

00:10:23.390 --> 00:10:26.330
of these measurement
sites across cities.

00:10:26.330 --> 00:10:28.440
In the city of San
Francisco, for example,

00:10:28.440 --> 00:10:31.220
there is one monitor
for the entire city.

00:10:34.100 --> 00:10:37.077
But in fact, now, thanks
to work that we've

00:10:37.077 --> 00:10:39.410
been doing for the last several
years, which I will talk

00:10:39.410 --> 00:10:43.520
about, it's critical that
we work towards a future

00:10:43.520 --> 00:10:46.910
where we have data and
information about emissions

00:10:46.910 --> 00:10:51.050
at a hyper-local scale
and providing data that

00:10:51.050 --> 00:10:54.120
is relevant and actionable.

00:10:54.120 --> 00:10:56.930
So Aclima has been working
for the last 10 years

00:10:56.930 --> 00:11:00.560
to really realize this vision
of ubiquitous air quality data

00:11:00.560 --> 00:11:03.950
and climate emissions data so
that we can really take action

00:11:03.950 --> 00:11:05.420
at the local scale.

00:11:05.420 --> 00:11:08.630
Air pollution, climate change,
these are global issues.

00:11:08.630 --> 00:11:11.840
But solutions happen locally in
our cities, and our counties,

00:11:11.840 --> 00:11:15.780
and our states, and
our communities.

00:11:15.780 --> 00:11:18.870
Solving the challenge of
actually delivering block

00:11:18.870 --> 00:11:22.620
by block data is an
incredibly difficult technical

00:11:22.620 --> 00:11:24.640
and scientific challenge.

00:11:24.640 --> 00:11:28.020
And so Aclima has invested
nearly 10 years of R and D

00:11:28.020 --> 00:11:30.880
into unlocking this capability.

00:11:30.880 --> 00:11:33.690
We started in 2008
and have really

00:11:33.690 --> 00:11:39.540
reached a monumental milestone
in the last couple of years

00:11:39.540 --> 00:11:41.910
with very strong
scientific validation

00:11:41.910 --> 00:11:46.410
behind our methodology and
acceptance of our methodology,

00:11:46.410 --> 00:11:48.570
broadly in the scientific
community, which

00:11:48.570 --> 00:11:53.400
really provides the basis for
accelerated global scaling.

00:11:53.400 --> 00:11:56.783
We've been working
with Google since 2010.

00:11:56.783 --> 00:11:58.200
And so our partnership
with Google

00:11:58.200 --> 00:11:59.575
has been evolving
over the years,

00:11:59.575 --> 00:12:03.530
and really excited about
where we are today.

00:12:03.530 --> 00:12:05.560
We deliver a unified platform.

00:12:05.560 --> 00:12:08.460
So we're delivering
best-in-class mobile and

00:12:08.460 --> 00:12:12.030
stationary sensors
that really work

00:12:12.030 --> 00:12:15.960
to deliver the highest
possible level of data quality

00:12:15.960 --> 00:12:17.880
from small scale sensors.

00:12:17.880 --> 00:12:20.910
We provide end-to-end
network management,

00:12:20.910 --> 00:12:24.090
so we manage the
entire network, and all

00:12:24.090 --> 00:12:25.770
of the data management
and analytics

00:12:25.770 --> 00:12:28.320
on the back end,
which runs on GCP.

00:12:28.320 --> 00:12:29.910
And we make all
of that available

00:12:29.910 --> 00:12:34.950
in an easy-to-access SAS
platform for our customers

00:12:34.950 --> 00:12:38.610
to really empower our customers
to take action to reduce

00:12:38.610 --> 00:12:40.980
emissions and reduce exposure.

00:12:44.250 --> 00:12:49.380
And really, one of the core
enablers of city scale mapping

00:12:49.380 --> 00:12:53.080
is our proven methodology that
leverages connected vehicles.

00:12:53.080 --> 00:12:55.380
So we're really taking
advantage of the trends

00:12:55.380 --> 00:13:00.240
in connected vehicles to use
roving fleets of vehicles

00:13:00.240 --> 00:13:02.190
as a roving sensor network.

00:13:02.190 --> 00:13:04.260
And we've done some
really fantastic work

00:13:04.260 --> 00:13:06.900
over the last several
years with Google,

00:13:06.900 --> 00:13:09.410
and Google Street View,
Google Earth Outreach,

00:13:09.410 --> 00:13:11.460
and a number of
scientific partners

00:13:11.460 --> 00:13:14.190
to validate this
methodology that

00:13:14.190 --> 00:13:19.120
results in representative
maps of hyper-local hotspots

00:13:19.120 --> 00:13:24.200
and sources as well
as areas of exposure.

00:13:24.200 --> 00:13:26.640
And so we're now
working with fleets

00:13:26.640 --> 00:13:29.250
to scale our data
collection platform,

00:13:29.250 --> 00:13:33.120
to scale the mapping platform
to deliver this data to cities

00:13:33.120 --> 00:13:35.360
around the world.

00:13:35.360 --> 00:13:37.780
And last year we
announced-- last September

00:13:37.780 --> 00:13:40.810
we announced that we're taking
our partnership with Google

00:13:40.810 --> 00:13:43.240
and Google Street
View Global, which

00:13:43.240 --> 00:13:47.560
is a really exciting development
in our journey with Google,

00:13:47.560 --> 00:13:51.580
which we're incredibly
just over the moon about,

00:13:51.580 --> 00:13:53.830
to really bring
this information,

00:13:53.830 --> 00:13:56.650
again, to millions and billions
of people around the world.

00:13:56.650 --> 00:13:59.230
You can actually see a
live demo of data streaming

00:13:59.230 --> 00:14:04.210
in from the Street View fleet
at the Bigtable showcase.

00:14:04.210 --> 00:14:06.910
And now we're
working with fleets

00:14:06.910 --> 00:14:09.760
beyond Street View
and other fleets

00:14:09.760 --> 00:14:12.310
to expand and scale
our efforts, which

00:14:12.310 --> 00:14:14.230
is why we're so
excited to be working

00:14:14.230 --> 00:14:17.200
with Geotab, one of the
leaders in fleet telematics.

00:14:17.200 --> 00:14:20.920
So with that, Mike, I'll
hand it over to you.

00:14:20.920 --> 00:14:21.910
Thank you.

00:14:27.365 --> 00:14:28.990
MIKE BRANCH: Thank
you so much, Davida.

00:14:28.990 --> 00:14:30.900
I'll just put this
mic down over here.

00:14:34.200 --> 00:14:38.240
So first of all, I'm really
excited to be here today

00:14:38.240 --> 00:14:40.760
to talk about two really
synergistic technologies

00:14:40.760 --> 00:14:42.680
that I think together
have the power

00:14:42.680 --> 00:14:45.360
to really be transformative.

00:14:45.360 --> 00:14:48.020
So quickly about Geotab, we
are the world's second largest

00:14:48.020 --> 00:14:50.120
telematics company.

00:14:50.120 --> 00:14:54.620
We have about 35,000 different
customers across the globe

00:14:54.620 --> 00:14:55.670
right now.

00:14:55.670 --> 00:14:58.010
And we are available
in commercial fleet.

00:14:58.010 --> 00:15:01.557
So a fleet might range in
size from 1 to 100,000.

00:15:01.557 --> 00:15:03.140
And typically, these
commercial fleets

00:15:03.140 --> 00:15:04.740
use us for a few
different purposes.

00:15:04.740 --> 00:15:06.937
So they'll use us from
a safety perspective,

00:15:06.937 --> 00:15:09.020
from a compliance perspective,
from a productivity

00:15:09.020 --> 00:15:10.410
perspective.

00:15:10.410 --> 00:15:11.660
And we've got a little device.

00:15:11.660 --> 00:15:13.190
And it plugs into
the vehicle into

00:15:13.190 --> 00:15:14.870
the onboard diagnostic port.

00:15:14.870 --> 00:15:17.090
And it tracks everything
from windshield wiper

00:15:17.090 --> 00:15:23.120
activation through to engine our
RPM, through to harsh braking.

00:15:23.120 --> 00:15:25.500
There's a little
accelerometer built in there.

00:15:25.500 --> 00:15:27.077
And we even got a gyro as well.

00:15:27.077 --> 00:15:28.910
One of the things that
we've got in the port

00:15:28.910 --> 00:15:31.670
of the little device
is the ability

00:15:31.670 --> 00:15:34.230
to connect up third
party sensors as well.

00:15:34.230 --> 00:15:37.130
So it really transforms
the commercial vehicle

00:15:37.130 --> 00:15:38.600
into this IoT hub.

00:15:38.600 --> 00:15:41.978
And all of this data streams
into the GCP platform.

00:15:41.978 --> 00:15:43.520
And what you're
seeing here is really

00:15:43.520 --> 00:15:45.380
a point cloud of a
single day's worth

00:15:45.380 --> 00:15:47.720
of driving of all
the fleets that are

00:15:47.720 --> 00:15:49.400
equipped with Geotab vehicles.

00:15:49.400 --> 00:15:51.830
And that's 1 and 1/2
million vehicles right now

00:15:51.830 --> 00:15:55.280
across the globe and
streaming in 30 billion data

00:15:55.280 --> 00:15:56.700
points on a daily basis.

00:15:56.700 --> 00:15:59.973
So we're doing that
all through GCP.

00:15:59.973 --> 00:16:01.390
So what's the
problem we're trying

00:16:01.390 --> 00:16:03.010
to solve for here right now?

00:16:03.010 --> 00:16:05.380
This isn't the traditional
fleet manager problem.

00:16:05.380 --> 00:16:07.370
But it's a little bit different.

00:16:07.370 --> 00:16:08.830
It's looking at a fleet.

00:16:08.830 --> 00:16:11.470
And how can I decide
which vehicles

00:16:11.470 --> 00:16:12.970
I'm going to place
these air quality

00:16:12.970 --> 00:16:15.820
sensors on such that I can
leverage this amazing solution

00:16:15.820 --> 00:16:17.960
that Aclima has created?

00:16:17.960 --> 00:16:21.250
But it's not only which
vehicles to put it on.

00:16:21.250 --> 00:16:22.030
It's which fleets.

00:16:22.030 --> 00:16:24.360
Which fleets are best
serviced for this?

00:16:24.360 --> 00:16:26.110
So in order determine
that, we've actually

00:16:26.110 --> 00:16:28.360
developed some really cool
machine learning algorithms

00:16:28.360 --> 00:16:31.057
that take the driving
patterns of vehicles

00:16:31.057 --> 00:16:32.890
and transform them into
different vocations.

00:16:32.890 --> 00:16:35.260
We've got 35,000 customers--

00:16:35.260 --> 00:16:38.180
garbage trucks, police
vehicles, couriers, you name it.

00:16:38.180 --> 00:16:40.720
So we have to first understand
how these vehicles are

00:16:40.720 --> 00:16:42.315
moving around cities.

00:16:42.315 --> 00:16:43.690
And once we were
able to do that,

00:16:43.690 --> 00:16:45.760
we actually ran an
interesting study

00:16:45.760 --> 00:16:47.260
with the Environmental
Defense Fund,

00:16:47.260 --> 00:16:49.840
which you can see through
this link over here.

00:16:49.840 --> 00:16:52.150
And what we were trying
to do is figure out,

00:16:52.150 --> 00:16:55.180
for a specific
geographic area, what

00:16:55.180 --> 00:16:57.250
are the types of
fleets that will best

00:16:57.250 --> 00:17:00.330
maximize geographic coverage?

00:17:00.330 --> 00:17:02.330
And again, it's all
available through that link.

00:17:02.330 --> 00:17:03.705
But what this is
showing you here

00:17:03.705 --> 00:17:05.547
is a story for Washington DC.

00:17:05.547 --> 00:17:07.630
And we ran it with one of
our customers, DC Water,

00:17:07.630 --> 00:17:08.380
at aggregate.

00:17:08.380 --> 00:17:10.880
And this is showing that, over
a period of about six months,

00:17:10.880 --> 00:17:17.349
we were able to get 70% coverage
of the entire geography of DC.

00:17:17.349 --> 00:17:18.925
And that's using
their public fleet,

00:17:18.925 --> 00:17:20.800
whereas you look at the
private sector fleets

00:17:20.800 --> 00:17:22.190
and you get about 50%.

00:17:22.190 --> 00:17:24.550
And although this is
representative for DC,

00:17:24.550 --> 00:17:26.349
we were seeing this
picture more or less

00:17:26.349 --> 00:17:29.788
across the board for small,
large municipalities.

00:17:29.788 --> 00:17:31.330
And when we talk
about coverage, it's

00:17:31.330 --> 00:17:33.220
really important to
understand what we mean

00:17:33.220 --> 00:17:35.080
when we say coverage as well.

00:17:35.080 --> 00:17:37.210
So we look at coverage
from a geohash perspective.

00:17:37.210 --> 00:17:39.543
And for those of you who
aren't familiar with geohashes,

00:17:39.543 --> 00:17:43.180
it's a way of dividing up the
earth into various quadrants.

00:17:43.180 --> 00:17:45.020
And we look at it at
the geohash 7 level,

00:17:45.020 --> 00:17:47.470
which is an alphanumeric
representation

00:17:47.470 --> 00:17:49.240
of a specific area.

00:17:49.240 --> 00:17:52.240
And the geohash 7 level
represents about 500 feet

00:17:52.240 --> 00:17:53.750
by 500 feet.

00:17:53.750 --> 00:17:55.690
And when we say
something is covered,

00:17:55.690 --> 00:17:58.390
it means it's been covered
by about 15 different passes

00:17:58.390 --> 00:18:00.930
through each of these geohashes.

00:18:00.930 --> 00:18:01.450
OK.

00:18:01.450 --> 00:18:05.720
And that was, again,
through the study with EDF.

00:18:05.720 --> 00:18:09.170
Turns out that having municipal
fleet vehicles collect

00:18:09.170 --> 00:18:12.378
these air quality sensor
readings is a good thing.

00:18:12.378 --> 00:18:14.420
It's good because there's
an incentive to deploy.

00:18:14.420 --> 00:18:17.340
There's a duty to serve
back to the public.

00:18:17.340 --> 00:18:19.970
We have so many different varied
use cases of these vehicles,

00:18:19.970 --> 00:18:20.470
too.

00:18:20.470 --> 00:18:23.450
You have public transit buses,
folks in water and wastewater.

00:18:23.450 --> 00:18:26.660
And the coverage is so disparate
that we get a really nice

00:18:26.660 --> 00:18:28.070
geographic spread.

00:18:28.070 --> 00:18:31.070
And then the third thing
is that there's always

00:18:31.070 --> 00:18:33.400
a link to the Environmental
and Sustainability Office.

00:18:33.400 --> 00:18:37.100
So we typically work with
the fleet operations.

00:18:37.100 --> 00:18:39.180
But you bring fleet
together with environment,

00:18:39.180 --> 00:18:42.100
and you really have a
great solution here.

00:18:42.100 --> 00:18:43.600
So what I'm going
to do is I'm going

00:18:43.600 --> 00:18:47.620
to give you an example of
a case study from DC Water.

00:18:47.620 --> 00:18:48.310
OK.

00:18:48.310 --> 00:18:50.350
And I will take Tim.

00:18:50.350 --> 00:18:52.010
Tim is a fleet
manager at DC Water.

00:18:52.010 --> 00:18:55.670
He's got about 600 different
vehicles under his control.

00:18:55.670 --> 00:18:56.387
OK.

00:18:56.387 --> 00:18:57.970
And he's been tasked
with figuring out

00:18:57.970 --> 00:19:00.910
which vehicles are
going to be best suited

00:19:00.910 --> 00:19:03.040
for looking at integration
of the Aclima platform,

00:19:03.040 --> 00:19:05.530
which of his vehicles he
needs to put these on.

00:19:05.530 --> 00:19:09.250
I mean, he's already inundated
with safety, and compliance,

00:19:09.250 --> 00:19:10.510
and productivity issues.

00:19:10.510 --> 00:19:12.430
He needs a simple tool
to be able to figure

00:19:12.430 --> 00:19:15.170
out which of these vehicles
that he needs to put this on.

00:19:15.170 --> 00:19:16.145
OK.

00:19:16.145 --> 00:19:17.770
And you might ask,
well, why doesn't he

00:19:17.770 --> 00:19:19.870
just outfit the entire
fleet of 600 vehicles?

00:19:19.870 --> 00:19:21.905
Well, first, that would
be cost prohibitive.

00:19:21.905 --> 00:19:23.530
And second of all,
we've proven that he

00:19:23.530 --> 00:19:24.610
doesn't need to do that.

00:19:24.610 --> 00:19:26.890
He can do it on a smaller
segment of vehicles

00:19:26.890 --> 00:19:30.260
to understanding their
movement patterns.

00:19:30.260 --> 00:19:34.880
Now imagine that you've
got thousands of Tims.

00:19:34.880 --> 00:19:36.840
We've got 35,000
customers, thousands

00:19:36.840 --> 00:19:38.090
in the municipal fleet sector.

00:19:38.090 --> 00:19:40.860
We need something that's going
to scale really, really well.

00:19:40.860 --> 00:19:44.220
We can't just do it for
Tim and expect to be done.

00:19:44.220 --> 00:19:47.010
So I'm going to walk you through
our process of ingesting all

00:19:47.010 --> 00:19:49.708
of this data,
transforming it, exploring

00:19:49.708 --> 00:19:51.000
it, which is really important--

00:19:51.000 --> 00:19:52.770
I think the most
important step--

00:19:52.770 --> 00:19:56.090
and then developing a solution
that works well, a very, very

00:19:56.090 --> 00:19:59.240
scalable approach.

00:19:59.240 --> 00:20:03.500
So I'm going to flip back about
two years, three years to 2016

00:20:03.500 --> 00:20:06.700
where we were monitoring
400,000 vehicles.

00:20:06.700 --> 00:20:08.710
The old way we looked
at this-- so the vehicle

00:20:08.710 --> 00:20:11.230
will communicate over
the cellular network.

00:20:11.230 --> 00:20:13.630
It will send data
through to our gateway

00:20:13.630 --> 00:20:15.853
servers, which sit on GCP.

00:20:15.853 --> 00:20:17.770
That then goes through
to our streaming server

00:20:17.770 --> 00:20:19.540
again on Compute Engine.

00:20:19.540 --> 00:20:22.330
And through streaming
to BigQuery,

00:20:22.330 --> 00:20:25.570
we're able to get
that data to BigQuery

00:20:25.570 --> 00:20:27.650
within 5 to 10 seconds.

00:20:27.650 --> 00:20:30.850
So for 400,000 vehicles,
from the time the data hits

00:20:30.850 --> 00:20:32.620
our gateway servers
to the time it's

00:20:32.620 --> 00:20:36.820
available in BigQuery is
about 5 to 10 seconds.

00:20:36.820 --> 00:20:41.175
Problem is, as we started to
approach a million vehicles,

00:20:41.175 --> 00:20:42.550
that really started
to slow down.

00:20:42.550 --> 00:20:44.467
So we've got these
dashboards across the board

00:20:44.467 --> 00:20:47.230
in our entire organization
that allow us to see what's

00:20:47.230 --> 00:20:48.580
going on within our ecosystem.

00:20:48.580 --> 00:20:50.920
And we were seeing that
5 to 10 seconds jump up

00:20:50.920 --> 00:20:55.090
to 11 minutes, 20
minutes, an hour.

00:20:55.090 --> 00:20:58.570
I mean, thank goodness
we had the foresight

00:20:58.570 --> 00:21:00.130
to see this coming about.

00:21:00.130 --> 00:21:03.210
And we decided a new
approach was really needed.

00:21:03.210 --> 00:21:05.210
And that new approach is
what we're doing today.

00:21:05.210 --> 00:21:07.450
So we've got about 1 and
1/2 million vehicles,

00:21:07.450 --> 00:21:10.090
again, streaming through
the cellular network

00:21:10.090 --> 00:21:12.970
to our gateway servers.

00:21:12.970 --> 00:21:16.090
Segmented files are sent
through to Cloud Storage.

00:21:16.090 --> 00:21:17.590
And then we've got
Kubernetes Engine

00:21:17.590 --> 00:21:18.882
that picks up all of this data.

00:21:18.882 --> 00:21:21.250
So we've got 36 different
pods in Kubernetes

00:21:21.250 --> 00:21:22.780
that are picking
up all this data

00:21:22.780 --> 00:21:25.720
and then pushing
it into BigQuery

00:21:25.720 --> 00:21:27.370
using streaming inserts.

00:21:27.370 --> 00:21:30.820
And then we're able to maintain
that 5 to 10 second lag.

00:21:30.820 --> 00:21:32.980
So that worked
exceptionally well for us.

00:21:32.980 --> 00:21:36.010
Kubernetes gave us the ability
to scale really, really well.

00:21:36.010 --> 00:21:39.838
And we can dynamically scale
it up and down as needed.

00:21:39.838 --> 00:21:41.380
So now that we've
done the ingestion,

00:21:41.380 --> 00:21:43.610
time comes for transformation.

00:21:43.610 --> 00:21:45.850
So I mention we've got 30
billion records coming in

00:21:45.850 --> 00:21:46.660
on a daily basis.

00:21:46.660 --> 00:21:50.542
About a third of that is GPS,
so 10 billion GPS records.

00:21:50.542 --> 00:21:52.250
Now, in order to
develop this tool that's

00:21:52.250 --> 00:21:54.700
going to be really useful to
be able to figure out which

00:21:54.700 --> 00:21:57.700
vehicles we want to
deploy, I don't need access

00:21:57.700 --> 00:21:58.690
to the raw data.

00:21:58.690 --> 00:22:01.250
I need access to some
transformed data.

00:22:01.250 --> 00:22:03.100
So then we
transformed this data.

00:22:03.100 --> 00:22:05.860
The raw data comes in
the form of the vehicle,

00:22:05.860 --> 00:22:07.150
the speed, the GPS.

00:22:07.150 --> 00:22:09.520
We're getting every single
entry, so 10 billion

00:22:09.520 --> 00:22:11.050
of these entries coming in.

00:22:11.050 --> 00:22:13.840
Then what we do is we throw
it into Cloud Composer.

00:22:13.840 --> 00:22:18.520
So essentially, we started
off with Apache Airflow,

00:22:18.520 --> 00:22:20.728
then moved to Composer
because it scaled really well.

00:22:20.728 --> 00:22:22.145
And then what we're
doing is we're

00:22:22.145 --> 00:22:25.000
taking the data from BigQuery
and essentially putting it back

00:22:25.000 --> 00:22:27.872
into BigQuery through Composer.

00:22:27.872 --> 00:22:28.830
That works really well.

00:22:28.830 --> 00:22:31.840
We use this process a lot
throughout the organization.

00:22:31.840 --> 00:22:34.450
And now we've transformed
it into a way where we've

00:22:34.450 --> 00:22:36.393
got the metrics by Geohash.

00:22:36.393 --> 00:22:37.810
And we can pull
those out directly

00:22:37.810 --> 00:22:42.400
from BigQuery in the
application that we push out.

00:22:42.400 --> 00:22:44.800
Exploration of data
is absolutely crucial.

00:22:44.800 --> 00:22:49.630
And BigQuery and BQGIS have
been phenomenal for this for us.

00:22:49.630 --> 00:22:54.250
So if anybody's done any
interpretation of GIS data

00:22:54.250 --> 00:22:56.740
at scale, you know it's
difficult to put a ton of data

00:22:56.740 --> 00:22:57.620
onto a map.

00:22:57.620 --> 00:23:00.250
So what we've done is we've
developed a tool leveraging

00:23:00.250 --> 00:23:04.570
deck.gl that allows us to put
millions of points on a map

00:23:04.570 --> 00:23:06.070
and be able to analyze that.

00:23:06.070 --> 00:23:08.030
So I can throw a
query into this--

00:23:08.030 --> 00:23:09.640
so this is the tool right here--

00:23:09.640 --> 00:23:12.490
into the side and then have
it visualized immediately.

00:23:12.490 --> 00:23:17.440
And this is great because all of
a sudden my data scientists who

00:23:17.440 --> 00:23:21.700
have been running Colab
and Jupyter Notebooks now

00:23:21.700 --> 00:23:24.400
have the ability to
write a single query

00:23:24.400 --> 00:23:27.760
with geospatial context and
visualize it immediately.

00:23:27.760 --> 00:23:29.680
All you need to
know is SQL, which

00:23:29.680 --> 00:23:31.360
is really, really powerful.

00:23:31.360 --> 00:23:33.190
And as a quick example
of what we did here

00:23:33.190 --> 00:23:35.770
during the exploration
process, we literally

00:23:35.770 --> 00:23:38.520
mapped all of the
coverage within DC.

00:23:38.520 --> 00:23:42.220
And we decided, wouldn't it be
interesting to take open data

00:23:42.220 --> 00:23:45.340
from DC and plot where all
the schools are, and see

00:23:45.340 --> 00:23:47.560
if we can find an
intersection, if we

00:23:47.560 --> 00:23:50.110
were to deploy
these sensors, where

00:23:50.110 --> 00:23:51.670
we would be missing
from a coverage

00:23:51.670 --> 00:23:52.880
perspective on schools?

00:23:52.880 --> 00:23:55.240
So we ran a quick
intersect statement

00:23:55.240 --> 00:23:57.070
over here leveraging SQL.

00:23:57.070 --> 00:23:59.680
And it pulled out
that data immediately.

00:23:59.680 --> 00:24:01.750
So again, leveraging
BigQuery, BQGIS

00:24:01.750 --> 00:24:06.900
has been transformational from
a data exploration perspective.

00:24:06.900 --> 00:24:08.290
And we didn't stop there.

00:24:08.290 --> 00:24:11.010
We wanted to understand
what kinds of vehicles

00:24:11.010 --> 00:24:12.810
would elicit the best coverage.

00:24:12.810 --> 00:24:15.900
And ideally, we've got
vehicles that would be electric

00:24:15.900 --> 00:24:20.490
or hybrid going across the
globe here to do these readings.

00:24:20.490 --> 00:24:22.620
But turns out, in
many fleets, that's

00:24:22.620 --> 00:24:23.845
still a small percentage.

00:24:23.845 --> 00:24:25.220
So if we were do
it for DC Water,

00:24:25.220 --> 00:24:28.590
we'd get about 14% coverage,
which isn't so great.

00:24:28.590 --> 00:24:31.810
We look at Diesel, we
get about 44% coverage,

00:24:31.810 --> 00:24:33.390
gas, 59% coverage.

00:24:33.390 --> 00:24:35.430
And if you look at a
consolidated approach,

00:24:35.430 --> 00:24:38.080
you get about 70% coverage.

00:24:38.080 --> 00:24:38.580
OK.

00:24:38.580 --> 00:24:41.925
So again, it does
warrant the need

00:24:41.925 --> 00:24:44.050
to have more of these hybrid
and electric vehicles,

00:24:44.050 --> 00:24:47.190
I think, within
communities for us

00:24:47.190 --> 00:24:49.937
to be able to do some
really cool stuff.

00:24:49.937 --> 00:24:52.270
So now that we've finished
this as we've done ingestion,

00:24:52.270 --> 00:24:54.930
we did transform, we did
explore, now it's time

00:24:54.930 --> 00:24:56.190
to actually develop something.

00:24:56.190 --> 00:24:57.090
OK?

00:24:57.090 --> 00:24:58.770
So what we've got
at Geotab, we've

00:24:58.770 --> 00:25:01.830
got a marketplace not
dissimilar to an app store.

00:25:01.830 --> 00:25:04.980
And I task our MVP team, which
is our minimum viable product

00:25:04.980 --> 00:25:07.440
team, to develop an application.

00:25:07.440 --> 00:25:09.570
And this was probably
about a two to three

00:25:09.570 --> 00:25:12.810
week initiative for them,
which was pretty incredible.

00:25:12.810 --> 00:25:15.930
And they were able
to leverage BQGIS

00:25:15.930 --> 00:25:19.290
and develop a tool that now
could go out to our 35,000

00:25:19.290 --> 00:25:23.040
different customers to allow
them to assess whether or not

00:25:23.040 --> 00:25:24.990
they've got good
coverage and which

00:25:24.990 --> 00:25:29.070
vehicles they need
to equip to be

00:25:29.070 --> 00:25:31.790
able to integrate with Aclima.

00:25:31.790 --> 00:25:34.380
And there are two primary
use cases that we looked at.

00:25:34.380 --> 00:25:35.775
One was for city-wide coverage.

00:25:35.775 --> 00:25:37.400
So it's looking at,
well, what coverage

00:25:37.400 --> 00:25:39.830
can I get from across
my entire city?

00:25:39.830 --> 00:25:41.593
Am I missing important areas?

00:25:41.593 --> 00:25:43.010
And these important
areas could be

00:25:43.010 --> 00:25:46.543
augmented with data available
from Aclima's system as well.

00:25:46.543 --> 00:25:47.960
And more importantly
as well, too,

00:25:47.960 --> 00:25:49.085
which areas aren't covered?

00:25:49.085 --> 00:25:50.630
Are there important
school zones,

00:25:50.630 --> 00:25:52.088
important areas
that aren't getting

00:25:52.088 --> 00:25:55.412
covered by this mapping?

00:25:55.412 --> 00:25:56.870
So what I'm going
to do is I'm just

00:25:56.870 --> 00:26:02.060
going to show you a quick
demo here of the application.

00:26:09.750 --> 00:26:12.070
All right.

00:26:12.070 --> 00:26:13.950
So what we're looking
at here is the add-in.

00:26:13.950 --> 00:26:17.610
And so we've got DC's kind
of boundaries drawn here.

00:26:17.610 --> 00:26:20.278
I'm going to run just a very
quick analysis for you here.

00:26:20.278 --> 00:26:22.320
So it's going to be from
October through December

00:26:22.320 --> 00:26:25.548
at an aggregate
level for DC Water.

00:26:25.548 --> 00:26:27.090
And what it's doing
in the back end--

00:26:27.090 --> 00:26:28.290
and I've staged some data here.

00:26:28.290 --> 00:26:29.490
That's why it was so quick--

00:26:29.490 --> 00:26:32.340
we're pulling off
queries from BigQuery,

00:26:32.340 --> 00:26:33.760
leveraging BQGIS as well.

00:26:33.760 --> 00:26:36.240
And what this is telling me
here now is, from October

00:26:36.240 --> 00:26:38.790
to December, I've
got 55% coverage

00:26:38.790 --> 00:26:41.400
with 30 vehicles in this area.

00:26:41.400 --> 00:26:43.590
Vehicle 8619, it's
a light duty truck.

00:26:43.590 --> 00:26:47.370
It represents about 81%
of that 55% coverage.

00:26:47.370 --> 00:26:52.750
If I zoom in here, I'm able
to quickly see each geohash.

00:26:52.750 --> 00:26:55.210
And each geohash that
is represented there

00:26:55.210 --> 00:26:58.150
means it's been covered, so
at least 15 or more passes.

00:26:58.150 --> 00:27:01.042
The lighter the color, the
fewer vehicles that were there.

00:27:01.042 --> 00:27:02.750
So you can clearly
see the major arteries

00:27:02.750 --> 00:27:04.375
by the number of
vehicles that are just

00:27:04.375 --> 00:27:06.190
shown in a deeper color.

00:27:06.190 --> 00:27:09.430
In addition, I've got this
pretty cool little chart

00:27:09.430 --> 00:27:12.070
over here that shows my
coverage percentage by number

00:27:12.070 --> 00:27:12.620
of vehicles.

00:27:12.620 --> 00:27:15.190
So if I have 30
vehicles, which I do,

00:27:15.190 --> 00:27:19.160
15 passage represents that 55%
that you were seeing up there.

00:27:19.160 --> 00:27:21.460
If I scroll up to here,
I get 50 vehicles.

00:27:21.460 --> 00:27:23.650
I get about 61% coverage.

00:27:23.650 --> 00:27:25.890
So I can look at
by number of passes

00:27:25.890 --> 00:27:28.880
and by number of
vehicles as well.

00:27:28.880 --> 00:27:31.177
Now let's say I
want to maybe look

00:27:31.177 --> 00:27:33.010
at-- this was for a
three month time period.

00:27:33.010 --> 00:27:35.385
Maybe I can get some better
data and some better coverage

00:27:35.385 --> 00:27:38.760
if I look at it over a
six month time period.

00:27:38.760 --> 00:27:43.370
So if I do that, I jump
up from 55% to about 65%

00:27:43.370 --> 00:27:45.300
here, which isn't too bad.

00:27:45.300 --> 00:27:49.040
What I can also do is I can have
a look at filtering this data

00:27:49.040 --> 00:27:50.240
a little bit more.

00:27:50.240 --> 00:27:52.282
So I could do it by number
of passes if I wanted.

00:27:52.282 --> 00:27:55.640
If I want to augment the
vehicles from 30 to 50 vehicles

00:27:55.640 --> 00:27:58.940
and apply that filter, you can
see that my percentage jumps up

00:27:58.940 --> 00:28:00.998
to about 70% coverage as well.

00:28:00.998 --> 00:28:03.290
So it becomes a business
decision at this point in time

00:28:03.290 --> 00:28:03.860
as well.

00:28:03.860 --> 00:28:08.570
Is it worth deploying another
20 vehicles for a 5% increase?

00:28:08.570 --> 00:28:10.910
Maybe I can leverage
some of the data

00:28:10.910 --> 00:28:14.940
available from Aclima's platform
to make up that difference as

00:28:14.940 --> 00:28:15.440
well, too.

00:28:15.440 --> 00:28:17.720
So it gives you a
good way to explore

00:28:17.720 --> 00:28:20.905
which vehicles are going to be
the best to give you coverage.

00:28:20.905 --> 00:28:22.280
The last thing I
want to show you

00:28:22.280 --> 00:28:25.700
here was that notion of looking
at which areas aren't covered.

00:28:25.700 --> 00:28:28.370
So we've imported,
again, the public school

00:28:28.370 --> 00:28:31.508
data from open DC.

00:28:31.508 --> 00:28:33.050
And if I click this
little icon here,

00:28:33.050 --> 00:28:36.530
we're running that
intersects query over BQGIS.

00:28:36.530 --> 00:28:38.660
And it's looking for an
intersection of areas

00:28:38.660 --> 00:28:40.130
where schools aren't covered.

00:28:40.130 --> 00:28:41.990
So I've identified a
handful of schools here

00:28:41.990 --> 00:28:45.180
that wouldn't be covered
if I were doing that.

00:28:45.180 --> 00:28:49.960
And if I looked at a major
kind of throughfare here,

00:28:49.960 --> 00:28:51.070
this is near a freeway.

00:28:51.070 --> 00:28:53.950
I could see that this
particular school here

00:28:53.950 --> 00:28:55.070
isn't immediately covered.

00:28:55.070 --> 00:28:56.487
Its surrounding
neighborhoods are.

00:28:56.487 --> 00:28:59.740
But it's River Terrace
Education, about 131 kids there

00:28:59.740 --> 00:29:01.300
that wouldn't have coverage.

00:29:01.300 --> 00:29:04.390
So one potential is you might
put a stationary sensor there.

00:29:04.390 --> 00:29:08.810
Or again, it might be some data
you could leverage from Aclima

00:29:08.810 --> 00:29:11.210
to make that complete coverage.

00:29:11.210 --> 00:29:14.050
And without further ado, I'm
going to bring Davida back

00:29:14.050 --> 00:29:14.667
on stage here.

00:29:14.667 --> 00:29:17.000
And she's going to talk to
us about the amazing solution

00:29:17.000 --> 00:29:18.600
that the folks at
Aclima have as well.

00:29:27.257 --> 00:29:28.090
DAVIDA HERZL: Great.

00:29:28.090 --> 00:29:29.400
Thank you, Mike.

00:29:29.400 --> 00:29:31.150
So when it comes to
measuring air quality,

00:29:31.150 --> 00:29:34.630
it's really important to
take a multi-pollutant view

00:29:34.630 --> 00:29:37.960
so that we can understand
emissions and pollution

00:29:37.960 --> 00:29:40.120
at a really granular level.

00:29:40.120 --> 00:29:41.770
Aclima measures
what matters when

00:29:41.770 --> 00:29:44.110
it comes to air pollution,
emissions, and climate change

00:29:44.110 --> 00:29:48.280
emissions, everything from
carbon dioxide and ozone,

00:29:48.280 --> 00:29:50.560
to black carbon, methane,
and particulate matter.

00:29:53.700 --> 00:29:57.780
We've been heavy users of
GCP for the past seven years.

00:29:57.780 --> 00:30:00.600
And we actually started
using Google Cloud tools

00:30:00.600 --> 00:30:03.150
before Google Cloud
was a separate group.

00:30:03.150 --> 00:30:08.670
So we've been really
dedicated users of GCP.

00:30:08.670 --> 00:30:12.600
And during that time, we built
a lot of custom infrastructure

00:30:12.600 --> 00:30:13.990
along the way.

00:30:13.990 --> 00:30:16.680
But we're really happy to
announce that we've now

00:30:16.680 --> 00:30:19.770
adopted GCP tools
across our stack,

00:30:19.770 --> 00:30:23.520
from BigQuery, and Cloud
Storage, Cloud SQL, Kubernetes,

00:30:23.520 --> 00:30:28.470
Google IoT, Pub/Sub, and
a whole breath of tools

00:30:28.470 --> 00:30:31.140
from the GCP platform,
which has really

00:30:31.140 --> 00:30:34.680
allowed us to focus on
what Aclima does best.

00:30:34.680 --> 00:30:37.530
And we've, in that
process, reduced

00:30:37.530 --> 00:30:40.200
the complexity of
our DevOps operation

00:30:40.200 --> 00:30:42.840
and reduced costs by 65%.

00:30:42.840 --> 00:30:45.420
And as heavy users
of BigQuery, we're

00:30:45.420 --> 00:30:48.300
taking advantage of
the new GIS functions

00:30:48.300 --> 00:30:51.480
to simplify complex
querying and aggregation

00:30:51.480 --> 00:30:54.690
to prepare for the
downstream analysis that

00:30:54.690 --> 00:30:56.590
occurs when we ingest
all of that data

00:30:56.590 --> 00:30:59.580
and then really work to help
our customers make sense

00:30:59.580 --> 00:31:01.720
of that data.

00:31:01.720 --> 00:31:04.050
And we're also really
happy to announce today

00:31:04.050 --> 00:31:07.620
that we are now a cloud
SAS partner, enabling

00:31:07.620 --> 00:31:09.540
us to bring the
best of everything

00:31:09.540 --> 00:31:13.237
that Aclima and Google have
to offer to our customers.

00:31:15.980 --> 00:31:20.120
Our services are offered
primarily through our SAS

00:31:20.120 --> 00:31:22.250
platform through Aclima Pro.

00:31:22.250 --> 00:31:24.590
Aclima Pro is a
paid premium service

00:31:24.590 --> 00:31:28.880
that allows public and private
sector customers in government

00:31:28.880 --> 00:31:34.400
and the enterprise to access,
analyze, query complex

00:31:34.400 --> 00:31:36.380
and dense air quality
and climate emissions

00:31:36.380 --> 00:31:41.450
data sets to better inform
a wide range of decisions

00:31:41.450 --> 00:31:46.860
to reduce emissions and
improve air quality.

00:31:46.860 --> 00:31:49.970
We also have a freemium
service that we

00:31:49.970 --> 00:31:52.130
offer to communities
and to citizens

00:31:52.130 --> 00:31:56.340
that turns all of that
complex information into data,

00:31:56.340 --> 00:31:59.420
or all of that complex
data into information

00:31:59.420 --> 00:32:04.520
that the public can consume
in a really intuitive way that

00:32:04.520 --> 00:32:07.880
enables broad public awareness
and engagement with air

00:32:07.880 --> 00:32:11.250
quality in our communities.

00:32:11.250 --> 00:32:14.070
The benefits of the
Aclima platform are broad.

00:32:14.070 --> 00:32:17.010
There is a broad
range of capabilities

00:32:17.010 --> 00:32:20.340
that the platform
enables our customers--

00:32:20.340 --> 00:32:23.500
empowers our customers to do.

00:32:23.500 --> 00:32:25.710
But fundamentally, it
comes down to really

00:32:25.710 --> 00:32:28.800
being able to diagnose
problems at the city scale.

00:32:28.800 --> 00:32:31.590
So I often say
that we're creating

00:32:31.590 --> 00:32:34.680
a wearable for the city
so that we can actually

00:32:34.680 --> 00:32:37.920
diagnose what's happening,
why it's happening,

00:32:37.920 --> 00:32:43.420
identify hotspots and
sources, reveal hotspots

00:32:43.420 --> 00:32:48.070
for human exposure so that then
we can actually take action,

00:32:48.070 --> 00:32:50.585
and once we take
action, understand

00:32:50.585 --> 00:32:52.210
the benefits of that
action, understand

00:32:52.210 --> 00:32:57.930
the ROI on the decisions
that we've taken as cities--

00:32:57.930 --> 00:33:00.700
as users of the platform.

00:33:00.700 --> 00:33:02.250
And so, as an
example-- and I think

00:33:02.250 --> 00:33:06.270
one of the most powerful
examples that we've seen,

00:33:06.270 --> 00:33:10.140
again, comes down to some of
our most impacted populations.

00:33:10.140 --> 00:33:13.080
So when you're a
city and you have

00:33:13.080 --> 00:33:15.630
to make a decision
about where to invest

00:33:15.630 --> 00:33:19.470
public funds to protect
school children, who are often

00:33:19.470 --> 00:33:21.810
the most affected by
pollution, they're

00:33:21.810 --> 00:33:25.590
having to do that in the blind,
so without any data whatsoever.

00:33:25.590 --> 00:33:28.650
But what the platform
enables our city users to do

00:33:28.650 --> 00:33:32.370
is to target those funds in
a way that really targets

00:33:32.370 --> 00:33:34.470
those areas that
are most impacted

00:33:34.470 --> 00:33:39.630
and benefits the broadest
number of citizens and children.

