00:36:47.230 --> 00:36:49.630
because I was setting up all
the computers in college.

00:36:49.630 --> 00:36:51.200
And really, it took
off from there.

00:36:51.200 --> 00:36:52.360
So I tell everybody,
if you to be

00:36:52.360 --> 00:36:54.700
the CIO of a government
someday, go to Humboldt State,

00:36:54.700 --> 00:36:56.860
get your degree in natural
resources planning,

00:36:56.860 --> 00:36:57.610
and you'll be set.

00:36:57.610 --> 00:37:00.580
You're on the track to be
a world famous CIO someday

00:37:00.580 --> 00:37:01.630
in government.

00:37:01.630 --> 00:37:06.040
But fast forward now,
25 years later to

00:37:06.040 --> 00:37:09.010
where we are today,
in San Mateo County,

00:37:09.010 --> 00:37:13.050
if you think about our county,
we're 800 square miles.

00:37:13.050 --> 00:37:13.580
Well, 500.

00:37:13.580 --> 00:37:14.080
Sorry.

00:37:14.080 --> 00:37:17.083
500 square miles, about
800,000 people in our county.

00:37:17.083 --> 00:37:19.000
For those of you that
aren't from around here,

00:37:19.000 --> 00:37:21.700
we're kind of the forgotten
county between San Francisco

00:37:21.700 --> 00:37:22.420
and San Mateo.

00:37:22.420 --> 00:37:25.090
So our county actually
goes all the way

00:37:25.090 --> 00:37:27.970
from the southern border
of San Francisco, which

00:37:27.970 --> 00:37:30.710
is about 50 square miles,
everything south of there--

00:37:30.710 --> 00:37:33.340
matter of fact, San Francisco
Airport is in my county.

00:37:33.340 --> 00:37:35.080
They pay us rent,
which we love--

00:37:35.080 --> 00:37:39.550
and goes all the way
down to Palo Alto.

00:37:39.550 --> 00:37:41.200
We get the poor side.

00:37:41.200 --> 00:37:42.310
We get East Palo Alto.

00:37:42.310 --> 00:37:43.950
They get Stanford
down in Santa Clara.

00:37:43.950 --> 00:37:46.280
But on the coast side,
it goes all the way down,

00:37:46.280 --> 00:37:47.600
almost to Santa Cruz.

00:37:47.600 --> 00:37:49.270
So we have a very
diverse county.

00:37:49.270 --> 00:37:52.720
On what we call the bay
side, the 101 corridor,

00:37:52.720 --> 00:37:56.560
you have 22 different cities
all down that corridor.

00:37:56.560 --> 00:37:59.827
Just amazing amount of
urban, really dense.

00:37:59.827 --> 00:38:02.410
Probably a lot of you that work
here in the Bay Area, probably

00:38:02.410 --> 00:38:04.630
your CEOs or founders
probably live in my county--

00:38:04.630 --> 00:38:06.290
Woodside, Atherton, Menlo Park.

00:38:06.290 --> 00:38:09.080
Those are all in my county.

00:38:09.080 --> 00:38:12.670
So we have this very
interesting, very dense,

00:38:12.670 --> 00:38:16.550
very technically savvy
engaged population.

00:38:16.550 --> 00:38:19.575
Now, if you go over 280, if
you've been down in our county,

00:38:19.575 --> 00:38:20.950
and you go to the
west side, it's

00:38:20.950 --> 00:38:22.810
all agricultural,
all mountainous, all

00:38:22.810 --> 00:38:23.440
very different.

00:38:23.440 --> 00:38:26.230
Now, early on in
the presentation,

00:38:26.230 --> 00:38:29.290
you saw a slide showing where
the sensor locations are.

00:38:29.290 --> 00:38:31.540
If you think it's challenging
for San Francisco, which

00:38:31.540 --> 00:38:34.400
is 50 square miles, to only
have one sensor in there county,

00:38:34.400 --> 00:38:35.890
we're 500 square miles.

00:38:35.890 --> 00:38:37.960
And I have one
sensor in my county.

00:38:37.960 --> 00:38:40.270
And it's on the bay
side in Redwood City.

00:38:40.270 --> 00:38:44.530
So if you live in Half Moon
Bay, which is on the coast,

00:38:44.530 --> 00:38:46.960
and you're trying to understand
what the air quality is

00:38:46.960 --> 00:38:50.350
like in your neighborhood based
on a sensor in Redwood City,

00:38:50.350 --> 00:38:53.230
you're probably not going to
see a really good correlation

00:38:53.230 --> 00:38:55.810
between your real
world experience

00:38:55.810 --> 00:38:58.930
and the stuff you're seeing as
the official air quality index.

00:38:58.930 --> 00:39:02.620
So that's how we got engaged
here with Google, and Aclima,

00:39:02.620 --> 00:39:06.280
and Geotab, because we were
convinced in the county

00:39:06.280 --> 00:39:08.170
that we needed to
engage partners

00:39:08.170 --> 00:39:10.390
like them to really
understand what

00:39:10.390 --> 00:39:12.430
was going on with
our environment

00:39:12.430 --> 00:39:15.770
to make things better for our
communities at a local level.

00:39:15.770 --> 00:39:18.850
So we launched about a year ago
now an initiative in our county

00:39:18.850 --> 00:39:20.590
called SMC Labs.

00:39:20.590 --> 00:39:22.780
Government, as some
of you may know,

00:39:22.780 --> 00:39:25.420
isn't known for
how fast we move.

00:39:25.420 --> 00:39:27.880
So we created this
innovation group.

00:39:27.880 --> 00:39:30.200
They engaged with our partners.

00:39:30.200 --> 00:39:32.020
We have signed an
agreement with them,

00:39:32.020 --> 00:39:33.770
which we're very excited about.

00:39:33.770 --> 00:39:37.510
We see this as the way to really
start understanding-- given

00:39:37.510 --> 00:39:39.580
the fact that we
have 22 cities, we

00:39:39.580 --> 00:39:41.740
have a huge fleet
of vehicles, we

00:39:41.740 --> 00:39:44.770
manage over 1,000 vehicles
for public safety,

00:39:44.770 --> 00:39:48.340
for public health, we
manage SamTrans vehicles

00:39:48.340 --> 00:39:50.590
that do the bus routes
all over our county,

00:39:50.590 --> 00:39:52.420
we think this is a
great opportunity

00:39:52.420 --> 00:39:55.960
to use a combination of
Geotab and Aclima data

00:39:55.960 --> 00:39:58.460
to really start understanding
what's going on in our county.

00:39:58.460 --> 00:40:00.560
Because these
problems we're trying

00:40:00.560 --> 00:40:02.750
to solve in the
real world, they're

00:40:02.750 --> 00:40:06.188
not specific to an invisible
political boundary.

00:40:06.188 --> 00:40:07.730
The problems around
air quality don't

00:40:07.730 --> 00:40:10.248
stop when you
cross from one city

00:40:10.248 --> 00:40:11.790
to another when
they're all together.

00:40:11.790 --> 00:40:13.920
There's really a
regional problem

00:40:13.920 --> 00:40:16.820
we're trying to solve--
we're trying to engage on.

00:40:16.820 --> 00:40:19.070
All of us probably have
loved ones or people

00:40:19.070 --> 00:40:20.810
we care about in schools.

00:40:20.810 --> 00:40:22.940
When we had the fires--

00:40:22.940 --> 00:40:24.680
one of my other
part time jobs, I'm

00:40:24.680 --> 00:40:26.520
a trustee on a school board.

00:40:26.520 --> 00:40:29.450
But when we were having the
fires up in the North Bay,

00:40:29.450 --> 00:40:31.070
that wasn't just a
North Bay problem.

00:40:31.070 --> 00:40:33.560
Granted, I mean, that was
horrific for the people

00:40:33.560 --> 00:40:34.580
up in the North Bay.

00:40:34.580 --> 00:40:36.500
But the huge plume of
smoke went offshore

00:40:36.500 --> 00:40:39.470
because of the wind currents
and came in over our county.

00:40:39.470 --> 00:40:42.050
And it was really
eye opening just

00:40:42.050 --> 00:40:45.380
to see how impactful
that was to how

00:40:45.380 --> 00:40:47.870
people perceive their quality
of life and things like that.

00:40:47.870 --> 00:40:52.220
There was people calling our
hospitals, sheltering in place.

00:40:52.220 --> 00:40:53.840
Schools were closing.

00:40:53.840 --> 00:40:57.920
I mean, it really caused
sort of this ancillary impact

00:40:57.920 --> 00:41:01.420
to the community, to our
whole region for a while,

00:41:01.420 --> 00:41:02.990
that I think you're
always concerned

00:41:02.990 --> 00:41:04.730
about the people who's
losing their houses, who's

00:41:04.730 --> 00:41:05.522
losing their lives.

00:41:05.522 --> 00:41:09.000
But it really brought home to
all of us how we're all in this

00:41:09.000 --> 00:41:09.500
together.

00:41:09.500 --> 00:41:11.375
And we have to solve
these problems together.

00:41:11.375 --> 00:41:14.450
And air quality is just, I
think, one of those really easy

00:41:14.450 --> 00:41:18.710
to understand, important
values that we can look at,

00:41:18.710 --> 00:41:20.000
that we can track.

00:41:20.000 --> 00:41:22.640
And I think-- my hope
is in the coming months,

00:41:22.640 --> 00:41:24.590
as we continue to
explore and expand

00:41:24.590 --> 00:41:28.632
this partnership with Google,
and with Aclima, and Geotab,

00:41:28.632 --> 00:41:30.590
and we start to deploy
more of this technology,

00:41:30.590 --> 00:41:33.500
as we start to do some of
this analysis, as we create

00:41:33.500 --> 00:41:36.410
these great interactive maps
and visualizations that we can

00:41:36.410 --> 00:41:40.220
share with politicians
and the community

00:41:40.220 --> 00:41:43.040
about what the current condition
is in their neighborhoods

00:41:43.040 --> 00:41:45.260
and what they can do, and
policies and actions we can

00:41:45.260 --> 00:41:48.380
take based on data,
that that's really

00:41:48.380 --> 00:41:51.110
going to start helping us make
changes in our communities

00:41:51.110 --> 00:41:54.227
here in the Bay Area, and
then launch to other places.

00:41:54.227 --> 00:41:55.560
I know this is already going on.

00:41:55.560 --> 00:41:59.270
We were just in Denver last week
at a Smart Cities Conference,

00:41:59.270 --> 00:42:00.670
and they're talking about it.

00:42:00.670 --> 00:42:04.190
I think this is just really
the tip of the iceberg where,

00:42:04.190 --> 00:42:05.850
in a few years,
this is just going

00:42:05.850 --> 00:42:07.130
to be the norm for everything.

00:42:07.130 --> 00:42:09.233
So I really appreciate
you all being here.

00:42:09.233 --> 00:42:10.650
You're the brains
behind all this.

00:42:10.650 --> 00:42:12.442
And I'm going to hand
it back over to Chad.

00:42:18.720 --> 00:42:20.100
CHAD JENNINGS: Alrighty.

00:42:20.100 --> 00:42:23.100
So I wanted to,
first off, thank you

00:42:23.100 --> 00:42:26.760
all for going with
us on the journey.

00:42:26.760 --> 00:42:30.080
We've handed the mic off
a whole bunch of times.

00:42:30.080 --> 00:42:32.280
In the handbook for
presenting at this conference,

00:42:32.280 --> 00:42:34.060
they say not to do that.

00:42:34.060 --> 00:42:36.598
But really, the story spanned
a whole bunch of organizations

00:42:36.598 --> 00:42:37.890
and a whole bunch of use cases.

00:42:37.890 --> 00:42:39.360
And it was really
important to make

00:42:39.360 --> 00:42:42.120
sure to bring John on so
that he could explain who

00:42:42.120 --> 00:42:44.520
the people are that
benefit from the maps

00:42:44.520 --> 00:42:46.980
that Mike and Davida are making.

00:42:46.980 --> 00:42:49.670
So thank you very
much for coming.

